### Tailwind-Utilities

- Map each variant to its complete class list with an object or `switch` (`{ sm: "px-2 py-1 text-xs", lg: "px-4 py-2 text-base" }`); only the lookup is dynamic, never the class string.
- Apply at most one utility per CSS property and variant to an element, choosing it with a conditional (`gridLayout ? "grid" : "flex"`): when two conflict, the one later in the generated stylesheet wins, whatever their order in the class list.
- Expose a component's styling variations as props that select a variant; where the component must also accept classes from its caller, merge them with the project's conflict-aware class-merging utility, so the caller's utility replaces the conflicting one deliberately.
- Prefer the project's class-joining utility over hand-concatenated strings for a conditional class list.
- Prefer extracting a class list repeated across files into a component or template partial over an `@apply` class; keep `@apply` for small, highly reusable elements (a button, a form control) in a project without components, since `@apply` everywhere is writing CSS again.
<!-- Project-specific / Tailwind-Utilities -->

### Tailwind-Theme

- Declare every design token (color, spacing, radius, font) in the project's Tailwind theme (`@theme`, or `theme` in the config file), so it generates its utilities.
- Use an arbitrary value (`top-[117px]`) only for a one-off outside the theme; a value repeated across elements is a theme token.
- Toggle dark mode by a class or attribute by overriding the `dark` variant where the project's Tailwind version configures it (`@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));` in the CSS entry point, or `darkMode` in the config file); without an override, `dark:` utilities follow `prefers-color-scheme`.
<!-- Project-specific / Tailwind-Theme -->
